270
00:48:57,840 --> 00:49:08,720
which are perfect layers of molybdenum and ruthenium, if I recall correctly, stacked on top of each other in many layers, and then the light bounces off of it perfectly.

271
00:49:08,720 --> 00:49:16,800
When we think about a lens, it's in a shape, and it focuses the light. This is like a mirror that's also a lens, so it's pretty complicated.

272
00:49:16,800 --> 00:49:29,760
Any defect in these super thinly deposited stacks will mess it up. Any curvature issues will mess it up. There are a lot of challenges with scaling the production. It's quite artisanal in this sense

273
00:49:29,760 --> 00:49:43,600
because you're not making tens of thousands of these a year, you're making hundreds, you're making thousands. 60 tools a year, 18 of these per tool, you’re still in the hundreds,

274
00:49:43,600 --> 00:49:57,200
of tools, or you're at the thousand number roughly for these lenses and projection optics. Then you step forward to the reticle stage, which is also something really crazy.

275
00:49:57,200 --> 00:50:07,360
This thing moves at, I want to say, nine Gs. It will shift nine Gs because as you step across a wafer, the tool will go... The wafer stage is complementary. It's the

276
00:50:07,360 --> 00:50:16,160
wafer part. You line these two things up. You're taking all the light through the lenses that's focused, and here's the reticle, here's the wafer.

277
00:50:16,160 --> 00:50:25,680
The reticle's moving one direction, the wafer's moving the other direction as it scans a 26x33 millimeter section of the wafer, and then it stops.

278
00:50:25,680 --> 00:50:37,040
It shifts over to another part of the wafer and does it again. It does that in just seconds. Each of them is moving at nine Gs in opposite directions. Each of these things is a wonder and marvel

279
00:50:37,040 --> 00:50:50,640
of chemistry, fabrication, mechanical engineering, and optical engineering, because you have to align all these things and make sure they're perfect. All of these things have crazy amounts

280
00:50:50,640 --> 00:50:58,640
of metrology because you have to perfectly test everything. If anything is messed up, the yield goes to zero, because this is such a finely tuned system.

281
00:50:58,640 --> 00:51:10,640
By the way, it's so large that you're building it in the factory in Eindhoven, Netherlands, and they're deconstructing it and shipping it on many planes to the customer site, and then you're

282
00:51:10,640 --> 00:51:19,760
reassembling it there and testing it again. That process takes many, many months. There are so many steps in the supply chain, whether it's Zeiss making their

283
00:51:19,760 --> 00:51:29,760
lenses and projection optics or Cymer, which is an ASML-owned company, making the EUV source. Each of these has its own complex supply chain. ASML has commented that their supply chain has

284
00:51:29,760 --> 00:51:37,040
over ten thousand people in it. Like individual suppliers? Yes. It might not be directly. It might be through Zeiss having so many suppliers

285
00:51:37,040 --> 00:51:51,920
and XYZ company having so many suppliers. If you just think about it, you're talking about two physically moving objects that are the size of a wafer, and it has to be accurate

286
00:51:51,920 --> 00:52:04,720
to the level of single-digit nanometers or even smaller because the entire system, the overlay, the layer-to-layer overlay variation, has to be on the order of 3 nanometers.

287
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:14,160
If the overlay is 3 nms, that means each individual part, the accuracy of its physical movement has to be even less than that. It has to be sub-one nanometer in most cases,

288
00:52:14,160 --> 00:52:27,920
because the error of these things stack up. There's no way to just snap your fingers and increase production. Things as simple as power. The US going from zero percent power growth to

289
00:52:27,920 --> 00:52:41,920
two percent power growth, even though China's already at thirty, was so hard for America to do. And that's a really simple supply chain with very few people in it who make difficult things.

290
00:52:41,920 --> 00:52:53,920
There are probably 100,000 electricians and people who work in the electricity supply chain, or more, in the US? When you look at ASML, they employ so few people.

291
00:52:53,920 --> 00:53:02,960
Carl Zeiss probably employs less than a thousand people working on this, and all of those people are super, super specialized. You can't just train random people up

292
00:53:02,960 --> 00:53:11,600
for this in the snap of a finger. You can't just get your entire supply chain to get galvanized. Nvidia's had to do a lot

293
00:53:11,600 --> 00:53:18,800
to get the entire supply chain to even deliver the capacity they're going to make this year. When you go talk to Anthropic, they're like, "We're short of TPUs, we're short

294
00:53:18,800 --> 00:53:25,640
of training, and we're short of GPUs." When you go talk to OpenAI, they're like, "We're short of these things." OpenAI and Anthropic know they need X.

295
00:53:25,640 --> 00:53:36,480
Nvidia is not quite as AGI-pilled. They're building X - 1. You go down the supply chain, everyone's doing X - 1. In some cases, they're doing X ÷ 2,

296
00:53:36,480 --> 00:53:53,680
because they're not AGI-pilled. You end up with this time lag for the whip to react. The AI-pilledness and the desire to increase production takes so long. Once they finally understand that they need

297
00:53:53,680 --> 00:54:01,200
to increase production rapidly… They think they understand. They think AI means we have to go from 60 to 100, in addition to the tools getting

298
00:54:01,200 --> 00:54:09,760
better and faster, the source getting higher power from 500 watts to 1,000, and all these other aspects of the supply chain advancing technically and increasing production.

299
00:54:09,760 --> 00:54:23,040
They think they're actually increasing production a lot. But if you flow through the numbers… What does Elon want? He wants 100 gigawatts a year in space by 2028 or 2029.

300
00:54:23,040 --> 00:54:35,440
Sam Altman wants 52 gigawatts a year by the end of the decade. Anthropic probably needs the same, and Google needs that. You go across the supply chain, and it's like, wait, no, the supply chain can't

301
00:54:35,440 --> 00:55:50,640
possibly build enough capacity for everyone to get what they want on the side of compute. I feel like in the data center supply chain for the last few years,

302
00:55:50,640 --> 00:56:00,400
people have been making arguments like, "We are bottlenecked by this specific thing, therefore AI compute can't scale more than X." But as you've written about, if the

303
00:56:00,400 --> 00:56:11,520
grid is a bottleneck, then we just do behind the meter on the site, we do gas turbines, et cetera. If that doesn't work, there are all these other alternatives that people fall back on.

304
00:56:11,520 --> 00:56:24,000
I want to ask whether we can imagine a similar thing happening in the semiconductor supply chain. If EUV becomes a bottleneck, what if we just went back to 7 nm and did what China

305
00:56:24,000 --> 00:56:36,320
is doing currently, producing 7 nm chips with multi-patterning with DUV machines? If you look at a 7 nm chip like the A100, there's been a lot of progress

306
00:56:36,320 --> 00:56:54,880
obviously from the A100 to the B100 or B200. How much of that progress is just numerics? If you just hold FP16 constant from A100 to B100. The B100 is a little over one petaflop, and the

307
00:56:54,880 --> 00:57:07,600
A100 is like 300 teraflops. Yeah, 312. Holding numerics constant, you have a 3x improvement from A100 to B100.

308
00:57:07,600 --> 00:57:16,960
Some of that is the process improvement, some of that is just the accelerator design improving, which we could replicate again in the future. It seems there's actually a very small effect

309
00:57:16,960 --> 00:57:31,040
from the process improving from 7nm to 4 nm. I don't know the numbers offhand but let's say there's 150k wafers per month of 3 nm and eventually similar amounts for 2 nm.

310
00:57:31,040 --> 00:57:45,280
But then there's a similar amount for 7 nm. If you have all those old wafers and there's maybe a 50% haircut because the bits per wafer area are 50% less or something,

311
00:57:45,280 --> 00:58:01,200
it doesn't seem that bad to just bring on 7 nm wafers if that gives you another fifty or hundred gigawatts. Tell me why that's naive. We potentially do go crazy enough that this

312
00:58:01,200 --> 00:58:13,440
happens because we just need incremental compute, and the compute is worth the higher cost and power of these chips. But it's also unlikely to a large extent

313
00:58:13,440 --> 00:58:31,680
because some of these are not fair comparisons. For example, from A100, which is 312 teraflops, to Blackwell, which is 1,000 or 2,000 FP16, and then Rubin is 5,000 or so FP16… It's not

314
00:58:31,680 --> 00:58:45,440
a fair comparison because these chips have vastly different design targets. With A100, Nvidia optimized for FP16 and BF16 numerics.

315
00:58:45,440 --> 00:58:53,840
When you look at Hopper, they didn't care as much about that; they cared about FP8. When you look at Rubin, they don’t care about FP16 and BF16 so much,

316
00:58:53,840 --> 00:59:14,480
they care mostly about FP4 and FP6. Numerics are what they've designed their chip for. Let's say we make a new chip design on 7 nm, optimized for the numerics of the modern day.

317
00:59:14,480 --> 00:59:23,200
The performance difference is still going to be much larger than the FLOPS difference you mentioned. Often it's easy to boil things down to FLOPS

318
00:59:23,200 --> 00:59:40,480
per watt or FLOPS per dollar, but that's not a fair comparison. Let's look at Kimi K2.5 and DeepSeek. When you look at those two models and

319
00:59:40,480 --> 00:59:50,960
their performance on Hopper versus Blackwell on very optimized software, you get vastly different performance. Most of this is not attributed to

320
00:59:50,960 --> 00:59:59,680
FLOPS or numerics, because those models are actually eight-bit. So it's not like Blackwells and Hopper are both optimized for eight-bit, and Blackwell is not

321
00:59:59,680 --> 01:00:14,160
really taking advantage of its four-bit there. The performance gulf is actually much larger. Sure it's one thing to shrink process technology and make the transistor smaller

322
01:00:14,160 --> 01:00:22,160
so each chip has X number of FLOPS, but you forget the big gating factor. These models don't run on a single chip. They run on hundreds of chips at a time.

323
01:00:22,160 --> 01:00:31,360
If you look at DeepSeek's production deployment, which is well over a year old now, they were running on 160 GPUs. That's what they serve production traffic on.

324
01:00:31,360 --> 01:00:40,960
They split the model across 160 GPUs. Every time you cross the barrier from one chip to another, there is an efficiency loss. You have to transmit over high-speed

325
01:00:40,960 --> 01:00:51,280
electrical SerDes, which brings a latency cost and a power cost. There are all these dynamics that hurt. As you shrink and shrink the process node, you've

326
01:00:51,280 --> 01:01:04,480
increased the amount of compute in a single chip. Now in-chip movement of data is at least tens of terabytes a second, if not hundreds of terabytes a second.

327
01:01:04,480 --> 01:01:13,520
Whereas between chips, you're on the order of a terabyte a second. Then you have this movement of data between chips that are super close to each other physically.

328
01:01:13,520 --> 01:01:20,720
You can only put so many chips close to each other physically, so you have to put chips in different racks. The movement of data between racks is on the order

329
01:01:20,720 --> 01:01:32,800
of hundreds of gigabits a second, 400 gig or 800 gig a second, so roughly 100 gigabytes a second. So you have this huge ladder: on-chip communication is super fast, within the

330
01:01:32,800 --> 01:01:43,280
rack is an order of magnitude slower, and outside the rack is an order of magnitude lower than that. As you break the bounds of chips, you end up with a performance loss.

331
01:01:43,280 --> 01:01:52,720
The reason I explain this is because when you look at Hopper versus Blackwell, even if both are using a rack's worth of chips, Hopper is significantly slower.

332
01:01:52,720 --> 01:02:06,400
The amount of performance you have leveraged to the task within each domain—tens of terabytes a second of communication between these processing elements versus terabytes a second between these

333
01:02:06,400 --> 01:02:19,120
processing elements—is much, much higher and therefore the performance is much higher. When you look at inference at 100 tokens a second for DeepSeek and Kimi K2.5,

334
01:02:19,120 --> 01:02:24,640
the performance difference between Hopper and Blackwell is on the order of 20x. It's not 2x or 3x like the FLOPS performance difference indicates,

335
01:02:24,640 --> 01:02:32,640
even though those are on the same process node. There are just differences in networking technologies and what they've worked on. You can translate some of these back,

336
01:02:32,640 --> 01:02:42,160
but when you look at what they're doing on 3 nm with Rubin, some of those things are simply not possible to do all the way back on A100, even if you make a new chip for 7 nm.

337
01:02:42,160 --> 01:02:50,880
There are certain architectural improvements you can port and certain ones you cannot. The performance difference is not just going to be the difference in FLOPS.

338
01:02:50,880 --> 01:03:00,000
It's in some senses cumulative between the difference in FLOPS per chip, networking speed between chips, how many FLOPS are on a chip versus a system, and memory

339
01:03:00,000 --> 01:03:10,720
bandwidth on a single chip versus an entire system. All of these things compound. Can I ask you a very naive question? The B200 now has two dies on a single chip,

340
01:03:10,720 --> 01:03:19,360
so you can get that bandwidth without having to go through NVLink or InfiniBand. Next year, Rubin Ultra will have four dies on one chip.

341
01:03:19,360 --> 01:03:32,720
What is preventing us from just doing that with an older… How many dies could you have on a single chip and still get these tens of terabytes a second? Even within Blackwell, there are differences in

342
01:03:32,720 --> 01:03:45,200
performance when you're communicating on the chip versus across the chips. Those bounds are obviously much smaller than when you're going out of the entire chip.

343
01:03:45,200 --> 01:03:54,400
When you scale the number of chips up, there is some performance loss. It's not perfect, but it is way better than different entire packages.

344
01:03:54,400 --> 01:04:07,360
How large can advanced packaging scale? The way Nvidia is doing it is CoWoS. Google, Broadcom, MediaTek, and Amazon's Trainium are all doing CoWoS.

345
01:04:07,360 --> 01:04:19,680
But actually you can go look back at what Tesla did with Dojo, which they cancelled and restarted. Dojo was a chip that was the size of an entire wafer.

346
01:04:19,680 --> 01:04:30,400
They had 25 chips on it. There were some tradeoffs. They couldn't put HBM on it. But the positive side was that they had 25 chips on it.

347
01:04:30,400 --> 01:04:41,360
To date, it is still probably the best chip for running convolutional neural networks. It's just not great at transformers because the shape of the chip, the memory, the arithmetic,

348
01:04:41,360 --> 01:04:52,800
and all these various specifications are just not well-suited for transformers. They're well-suited for CNNs. Dojo chips were optimized around that, and they made a bigger package.

349
01:04:52,800 --> 01:05:03,520
But as you make packages bigger and bigger, you have other constraints: networking speed, memory bandwidth, and cooling capabilities. All of these things start to rear their heads.

350
01:05:03,520 --> 01:05:11,600
It's not simple. But yes, you will see a trend line of more chips on the package, and yes, you're going to be able to do that on 7 nm. In fact, that's what Huawei did

351
01:05:11,600 --> 01:05:23,360
with their Ascend 910C or D. They initially put one, and then they did two. They're focusing on scaling the packaging up because that is an

352
01:05:23,360 --> 01:05:32,000
area where they can advance faster than process technology where they can't shrink. But at the end of the day, that’s something you can do on the leading-edge chips too.

353
01:05:32,000 --> 01:05:42,880
Anything you do on 7 nm, you can also probably do on 3 nm in terms of packaging. If we end up in this world in 2030 where the West has the most advanced process technology

354
01:05:42,880 --> 01:05:53,680
but has not ramped it up as much, whereas China… I don't know if you think by 2030 they would have EUV and 2 nm or whatever. But they are semiconductor-pilled and they

355
01:05:53,680 --> 01:06:03,040
are producing in mass quantity. Basically, I'm wondering what the year is where there's a crossover, where our advantage in process technology has faded enough,

356
01:06:03,040 --> 01:06:13,600
and their advantage in scale has increased enough. And also, if their advantage in having one country with the entire supply chain indigenized—rather than having random suppliers in Germany

357
01:06:13,600 --> 01:06:28,640
and the Netherlands—would mean that China would be ahead in its ability to produce mass flops. To date, China still does not have an entirely indigenized semiconductor supply chain.

358
01:06:28,640 --> 01:06:47,200
But would they in 2030? By 2030, it's possible that they do. But to date, all of China's 7 nm and 14 nm capacity uses ASML DUV tools. The amount that they can import from ASML is large.

359
01:06:47,200 --> 01:06:58,640
But the vast majority of ASML's revenue, especially on EUV all of it, is outside of China. The scale advantage is still in the favor of the West plus Taiwan,

360
01:06:58,640 --> 01:07:03,040
Japan, and Korea, et cetera. But they're trying to make their own DUV and EUV tools, right? They're trying to do all these things.

361
01:07:03,040 --> 01:07:12,160
The question is how fast can they advance and scale up production as well as quality. To date, we haven't seen that. Now I'm quite bullish that they're

362
01:07:12,160 --> 01:07:20,000
going to be able to do these things over the next five to ten years. They will really scale up production and kick it into high gear.

363
01:07:20,000 --> 01:07:28,960
They have more engineers working on it and more desire to throw capital at the problem. So by 2030, will they have fully indigenized DUV? I think for sure. DUV, yes.

364
01:07:28,960 --> 01:07:42,160
And fully indigenized EUV by 2030? I think they'll have working tools. I don't think that they'll be able to manufacture a bunch yet. There's having it work, and then there's production hell.

365
01:07:42,160 --> 01:07:57,120
ASML had EUV working in the early 2010s at some capacity. The tools were not accurate enough. They were not scaled for high-volume manufacturing or reliable enough. They had to ramp production,

366
01:07:57,120 --> 01:08:07,360
and that all took time. Production hell takes time. That's why it took another five to seven years to get EUV into mass production at a fab rather than just working in the lab.

367
01:08:07,360 --> 01:08:23,600
How many DUV tools do you think they'll be able to manufacture in 2030? ASML? No, China. That's a great question. It's a bit of a challenge to look into this supply chain

368
01:08:23,600 --> 01:08:36,800
especially. We try really hard. In some instances, they're buying stuff from Japanese vendors. If they want a fully indigenized supply chain, they need to not buy these lenses, projection

369
01:08:36,800 --> 01:08:42,720
optics, or stages from Japanese vendors. They need to build it internally. It's really tough to say where they'll be able to get to.

370
01:08:42,720 --> 01:08:51,279
I honestly think it's a shot in the dark. But it's probably not unlikely that they'll be able to do on the order of 100 DUV tools a year, whereas ASML is currently doing

371
01:08:51,279 --> 01:09:12,319
hundreds of DUV tools a year. No company has a process node where they make a million wafers a month. Elon says he wants to do it and China is obviously going to do it. TSMC is trying to do that.

372
01:09:12,319 --> 01:09:22,880
The memory makers may get to a million wafers a month as well, but not in a single fab. It's mind-boggling to think of that scale, and challenging to

373
01:09:22,880 --> 01:09:34,319
see the supply chain galvanized for that. I don't want to doubt China's capability to scale. I guess this is an interesting question. I think at some point SemiAnalysis

374
01:09:34,319 --> 01:09:52,080
will do the deep dive on this. By when would indigenized Chinese production be bigger than the rest of the West combined. And put in the input of your model of when they'll

375
01:09:52,080 --> 01:10:00,720
have DUV machines and EUV machines at scale? Because there's this question around if you have long timelines on AI—by long meaning 2035, which is not that long in the grand

376
01:10:00,720 --> 01:10:09,360
scheme of things—should you expect a world where China is dominating in semiconductors? It doesn't get asked enough because if you're in San Francisco,

377
01:10:09,360 --> 01:10:19,360
we're thinking on timescales of weeks. If you're outside of San Francisco, you're not thinking about AGI at all. What if we have AGI? What if you have this transformational

378
01:10:19,360 --> 01:10:33,040
thing that is commanding tens or hundreds of trillions of dollars of economic growth and token output, but it happens in 2035? What does that imply for the West versus China?

379
01:10:33,040 --> 01:10:43,280
SemiAnalysis has got to write the definitive model on this. It's really challenging when you move timescales out that far.

380
01:10:43,280 --> 01:10:54,400
What we tend to focus on is tracking every data center, every fab, and all the tools. We track where they're going, but the time lags for these things are relatively short.

381
01:10:54,400 --> 01:11:04,720
We can only make reasonably accurate estimates for data center capacity based on land purchasing, permits, and turbine purchasing. We know where all these things

382
01:11:04,720 --> 01:11:19,760
are going, that's the data we sell. As you go out to 2035, things are just so radically different. Your error bars get so large it's hard to make an estimate. But at the end of the day, if takeoff

383
01:11:19,760 --> 01:11:36,560
or timelines are slow enough, I don't see why China wouldn't be able to catch up drastically. In some sense, we've got this valley where, three to six months ago, or maybe even now, Chinese

384
01:11:36,560 --> 01:11:45,920
models are as competitive as they've ever been. I think Opus 4.6 and GPT 5.4 have really pulled away and made the gap a little bit bigger, but I'm sure some new Chinese models will come out.

385
01:11:45,920 --> 01:11:59,040
As we move from selling tokens where they provide the entire reasoning chain, to selling automated white-collar work—an automated software engineer, you send them the request,

386
01:11:59,040 --> 01:12:05,907
they give you the result back, and there's a bunch of thinking on the back end that they don't show you—the ability to distill out of American models into Chinese models will be harder.

387
01:12:05,907 --> 01:12:17,040
Second, look at the scale of the compute the labs have. OpenAI exited the year with roughly two gigawatts last year. Anthropic will get to two-plus gigawatts this year.

388
01:12:17,040 --> 01:12:25,520
By the end of next year, they'll both be at ten gigawatts of capacity. China is not scaling their AI lab compute nearly as fast.

389
01:12:25,520 --> 01:12:37,280
At some point, when you can't distill the learnings from these labs into the Chinese models, plus with this compute race that OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Meta are all racing on,

390
01:12:37,280 --> 01:12:53,360
they end up getting to a point where the model performance should start to diverge more. Then look at all this CapEx being spent on data centers. Amazon is spending $200 billion, Google $180 billion.

391
01:12:53,360 --> 01:13:02,000
All these companies are spending hundreds of billions of dollars on CapEx. There's nearly a trillion dollars of CapEx being invested in data

392
01:13:02,000 --> 01:13:14,640
centers in America this year, roughly. What's the return on invested capital here? You and I would think the return on invested capital for data center CapEx is very high.

393
01:13:14,640 --> 01:13:24,240
If we look at Anthropic's revenues, in January they added $4 billion. In February, which was a shorter month, they added $6 billion. We'll see what they can do in March and April,

394
01:13:24,240 --> 01:13:31,120
given that compute constraints are what's bottlenecking their growth. The reliability of Claude is quite low because they're so compute constrained.

395
01:13:31,120 --> 01:13:42,640
But if this continues, then the ROIC on these data centers is super high. At some point, the US economy starts growing faster and faster over this year and next year

396
01:13:42,640 --> 01:13:54,400
because of all this CapEx, all the revenue these models are generating, and the downstream supply chain. China doesn't have that yet. They have not built the scale of infrastructure

397
01:13:54,400 --> 01:14:05,360
to invest in models, get to the capabilities, and then deploy these models at such scale. When you look at Anthropic, they're at $20 billion ARR.

398
01:14:05,360 --> 01:14:16,560
The margins are sub-50 percent, at least as last reported by The Information. So that's $13 or $14 billion of compute that it's running on rental cost-wise, which is actually $50

399
01:14:16,560 --> 01:14:28,800
billion worth of CapEx that someone laid out for Anthropic to generate their current revenue. China has just not done this. If and when Anthropic 10Xs revenue again—and

400
01:14:28,800 --> 01:14:39,360
I think our answer would be when, not if—China doesn't have the compute to deploy at that scale. So there is some sense that we're in a fast takeoff.

401
01:14:39,360 --> 01:14:47,760
It's not like we're talking about a Dyson sphere by X date, it's more like the revenue is compounding at such a rate that it does affect economic growth.

402
01:14:47,760 --> 01:14:56,160
The resources these labs are gathering are growing so fast. China hasn't done that yet, so in that case, the US and the West are actually diverging.

403
01:14:56,160 --> 01:15:05,200
The flip side is that these infrastructure investments have middling returns. Maybe they're not as good as hoped. Maybe Google is wrong for wanting

404
01:15:05,200 --> 01:15:13,840
to take free cash flow to zero and spend $300 billion on CapEx next year. Maybe they’re just wrong and people on Wall Street who are bearish and people

405
01:15:13,840 --> 01:15:23,840
who don't understand AI are correct. In that case, the US is building all this capacity but doesn't get great returns. Meanwhile, China is able to build a fully

406
01:15:23,840 --> 01:15:40,800
vertical, indigenized supply chain, instead of the US/Japan/Korea/Taiwan/SE Asia/Europe countries together building this less vertical supply chain. In a sense, at some point China is able to scale

407
01:15:40,800 --> 01:15:50,480
past us if AI takes longer to get to certain capability levels than the vast majority of your guests on this podcast believe. It's fast timelines, the US wins;

408
01:15:50,480 --> 01:16:01,040
long timelines, China wins. Yeah but I don't know what fast timelines means. I don't think you have to believe in AGI to have the timelines where the US wins.

409
01:16:01,040 --> 01:16:10,240
Let's go back to memory. I think people on Wall Street and people in the industry are understanding how big this is, but maybe generally people don't understand what a big deal it is.

410
01:16:10,240 --> 01:16:16,320
So we've got this memory crunch, as you were talking about. And earlier I was asking about, oh, could we solve for the EUV

411
01:16:16,320 --> 01:16:26,640
tool shortage by going back to seven nanometers? So let me ask a similar question about memory. HBM is made of DRAM, but has three to four times fewer bits per wafer

412
01:16:26,640 --> 01:16:35,760
area than the DRAM it's made out of. Is it possible that accelerators in the future could just use commodity DRAM and not HBM, so we can get

413
01:16:35,760 --> 01:16:48,560
much more capacity out of the DRAM we have? The reason I think this might be possible is, if we're going to have agents that are just going off and doing work, and it's

414
01:16:48,560 --> 01:17:04,080
not a synchronous chatbot application, then you don't necessarily need extremely fast latency. Maybe you can have lower bandwidth, because the reason you stack DRAM into

415
01:17:04,080 --> 01:17:17,680
HBM is for higher bandwidth. Is it possible to go to HBM accelerators and basically have the opposite of Claude Code Fast, like have Claude Slow?

416
01:17:17,680 --> 01:17:26,960
At the end of the day, the incremental purchaser who's willing to pay the highest price for tokens also ends up being the one that's less price-sensitive.

417
01:17:26,960 --> 01:17:35,040
Compute should be allocated, in a capitalistic society, towards the goods that have the highest value, and the private market determines this by willingness to pay.

418
01:17:35,040 --> 01:17:46,000
To some extent, Anthropic could actually release a slow mode. They could release Claude Slow Mode and increase tokens per dollar by a significant amount.

419
01:17:46,000 --> 01:17:59,120
They could probably reduce the price of Opus 4.6 by 4-5x and reduce the speed by maybe just 2x. The curve on inference throughput versus speed is already there just on HBM.

420
01:17:59,120 --> 01:18:11,360
And yet they don't, because no one actually wants to use a slow model. Furthermore, on these agentic tasks, it's great that the model can run at a time horizon of hours.

421
01:18:11,360 --> 01:18:21,920
But if the model was running slower, those hours would become a day. Vice versa, if the model is running faster, those hours become an hour.

422
01:18:21,920 --> 01:18:34,160
No one really wants to move to a day-long wait period, because the highest-value tasks also have some time sensitivity to them. I struggle to see… Yes,

423
01:18:34,160 --> 01:18:52,000
you could use regular DRAM. There are a couple of challenges with this. One of the core constraints of chips is that a chip is a certain size, and all

424
01:18:52,000 --> 01:19:02,320
of the I/O escapes on the edges. Often, the left and right of the chip are HBM—so the I/O from the chip to the HBM is on the sides—and then the

425
01:19:02,320 --> 01:19:17,120
top and bottom are I/O to other chips. If you were to change from HBM to DDR, all of a sudden this I/O on the edge would have significantly less bandwidth,

426
01:19:17,120 --> 01:19:34,000
but significantly more capacity per chip. But the metric you actually care about is bandwidth per wafer, not bits per wafer. Because the thing that is constraining the FLOPS

427
01:19:34,000 --> 01:19:44,160
is just getting in and out the next matrix, and for that you just need more bandwidth. Yeah, getting out the weights and getting in and out the KV cache.

428
01:19:44,160 --> 01:19:52,240
In many cases, these GPUs are not running at full memory capacity. It's obviously a system design thing: model, hardware, and software co-design.

429
01:19:52,240 --> 01:20:00,240
You have to figure out how much KV cache you need, how much you keep on the chip, how much you offload to other chips and call when you need it for tool calling,

430
01:20:00,240 --> 01:20:09,440
and how many chips you parallelize this on. Obviously, the search space for this is very broad, which is why we have InferenceX, an open-source model that searches all

431
01:20:09,440 --> 01:20:22,480
the optimal points on inference for a variety of different chips and models. The point is, you're not always necessarily constrained by memory capacity.

432
01:20:22,480 --> 01:20:33,440
You can be constrained by FLOPS, network bandwidth, memory bandwidth, or memory capacity. If you really simplify it down, there are four constraints,

433
01:20:33,440 --> 01:20:44,160
and each of these can break out into more. If you switch to DDR, yes, you produce four times the bits per DRAM wafer, but all of a sudden the constraints shift a lot and your

434
01:20:44,160 --> 01:20:53,520
system design shifts. You go slower. Is the market smaller? Maybe. But also, all these FLOPS are wasted because they're just sitting there waiting for memory.

435
01:20:53,520 --> 01:21:04,080
You don't need all that capacity because you can't really increase batch size because then the KV cache would take even longer to read. Makes sense. What is the bandwidth

436
01:21:04,080 --> 01:21:16,720
difference between HBM and normal DRAM? An HBM4 stack—let's talk about the stuff that's in Rubin, because that's what we've been indexing on—is 2048 bits across, connected in an

437
01:21:16,720 --> 01:21:27,680
area that's 13 millimeters wide. It transfers memory at around 10 giga-transfers a second. So a stack of HBM4 is 2048 bits on

438
01:21:27,680 --> 01:21:39,440
an area that's roughly 11 to 13 millimeters wide. That's the shoreline you're taking on the chip. In that shoreline, you have 2048 bits transferring at 10 giga-transfers per second.

439
01:21:39,440 --> 01:21:53,680
You multiply those together and divide by eight, bits to a byte, and you're at roughly 2.5 terabytes a second per HBM stack. When you look at DDR, in that same area, it's maybe 64 or 128 bits wide.

440
01:21:53,680 --> 01:22:07,760
That DDR5 is transferring at anywhere from 6.4 to maybe 8,000 giga-transfers a second. So your bandwidth is significantly lower. It's 64 times 8,000 divided by

441
01:22:07,760 --> 01:22:18,800
eight, which puts you at 64 gigabytes a second. Even if you take a generous interpretation of 128 times 8 giga-transfers, you're at 128 gigabytes a second for the same shoreline,

442
01:22:18,800 --> 01:22:27,520
versus 2.5 terabytes a second. There's an order of magnitude difference in bandwidth per edge area. If your chip is a square, or 26 by 33

443
01:22:27,520 --> 01:22:34,480
millimeters—which is the maximum size for an individual die—you only have so much edge area. On the inside of that chip, you put all your compute.

444
01:22:34,480 --> 01:22:42,800
There are things you can do to try and change that, like more SRAM or more caching. But at the end of the day, you're very constrained by bandwidth.

445
01:22:42,800 --> 01:22:52,080
Then there's the question of where you can destroy demand to free up enough for AI. I guess the picture is especially bad because, as you're saying, if it takes four times more

446
01:22:52,080 --> 01:23:08,160
wafer area to get the same byte, for HBM you have to destroy four times as much consumer demand for laptops and phones to free up one byte for AI. What does this imply for the next year or two?

447
01:23:08,160 --> 01:23:16,080
Sorry for the run-on question, in your newsletter you said 30% of Big Tech's CapEx in 2026 is going towards memory? Yes.

448
01:23:16,080 --> 01:23:26,480
That's insane, right? Of the $600 billion or whatever, 30% is going just to memory. Yes. Obviously, there's some level of margin stacking that Nvidia does,

449
01:23:26,480 --> 01:23:33,440
so you have to separate that out and apply their margin to the memory and the logic. But at the end of the day, a third of their CapEx is going to memory.

450
01:23:33,440 --> 01:23:48,080
That's crazy. What should we expect over the next year or two as this memory crunch hits? The memory crunch will continue to get harder, and prices will continue to go up.

451
01:23:48,080 --> 01:23:56,640
This affects different parts of the market differently. Are people going to hate AI more and more? Yes, because smartphones and PCs are not

452
01:23:56,640 --> 01:24:04,240
going to get incrementally better year on year. In fact, they're going to get incrementally worse. If you look at the bill of materials for an iPhone, what fraction of it is the memory?

453
01:24:04,240 --> 01:24:17,280
How much more expensive does an iPhone get if the memory is two times more expensive? I believe an iPhone has 12 gigabytes of memory. Each gig used to cost roughly $3-4, so that's $50.

454
01:24:17,280 --> 01:24:30,160
But now the price of memory has tripled. Let's say it's $12 per gig for DDR. Now you're talking about $150 versus $50. That's a $100 increase in cost for Apple.

455
01:24:30,160 --> 01:24:33,600
Apple has some margin, they're not just going to eat the margin. NAND also has the same market dynamics, so in reality, it's probably a $150 increase on the iPhone. So now that’s a $100 cost

456
01:24:33,600 --> 01:24:46,320
increase and that’s just on the DRAM. The NAND also has the same sort of market. So in fact it’s probably a $150 increase on the iPhone. Apple either has to pass that on to the consumer or eat it.

457
01:24:46,320 --> 01:24:54,880
I don't see Apple reducing their margin too much, maybe they eat a little bit. But at the end of the day, that means the end consumer is paying $250 more for an iPhone.

458
01:24:54,880 --> 01:25:06,480
Now that’s just on last year’s pricing versus today’s. There is some lag before Apple feels the heat because they tend to have long-term contracts

459
01:25:06,480 --> 01:25:13,360
for memory that last three months to a year. But at the end of the day, Apple gets hit pretty hard by this. They won't really

460
01:25:13,360 --> 01:25:20,640
adjust until the next iPhone release. But that's the high end of the market, which is only a few hundred million phones a year. Apple sells two or three hundred million

461
01:25:20,640 --> 01:25:31,520
phones annually. The bulk of the market is mid-range and low-end. It used to be that 1.4 billion smartphones were sold a year. Now we're at about 1.1 billion. Our projections are that we might

462
01:25:31,520 --> 01:25:42,880
drop to 800 million this year, and down to 500 or 600 million next year. We look at data points out of China from some of our analysts in Asia,

463
01:25:42,880 --> 01:25:52,720
Singapore, Hong Kong, and Taiwan. They've been tracking this, and they see Xiaomi and Oppo cutting low-end and mid-range smartphone volumes by half.

464
01:25:52,720 --> 01:26:08,720
Yes, it’s only a $150 BOM increase on a $1,000 iPhone where Apple has some larger margin. But for smaller phones, the percentage of the BOM that goes to memory and storage is much larger.

465
01:26:08,720 --> 01:26:20,080
And the margins are lower, so there's less capacity to even eat the margins. And they have also generally tended not to do long-term agreements on memory.

466
01:26:20,080 --> 01:26:32,240
Why this is a big deal is that if smartphone volumes halve, that drop will happen in the low and mid-range, not the high end. So it’s not like the bits released are halving.

467
01:26:32,240 --> 01:26:38,240
Currently, consumer devices account for more than half of memory demand. Even if you halve smartphone volumes, because of the shape of the halving,

468
01:26:38,240 --> 01:26:46,000
the low end gets cut by more than half, while the high end gets cut by less than half, because you and I will still buy the high-end phones that cost north of a thousand dollars.

469
01:26:46,000 --> 01:26:52,640
We'll buy them even if they get a little bit more expensive. And Apple's volumes will not go down as much as a low-end smartphone provider.

470
01:26:52,640 --> 01:27:06,240
The same applies to PCs. What this does to the market is quite drastic. DRAM gets released and goes to AI chips, who are willing to do longer-term contracts and pay higher

471
01:27:06,240 --> 01:27:22,400
margins, because at the end of the day the margin they extract from the end user is much larger. This probably leads to people hating AI even more. Today, you already see all the memes on PC

472
01:27:22,400 --> 01:27:33,440
subreddits and gaming PC Twitter. It's cat dancing videos saying, "This is why memory prices have doubled and you can't get a new gaming GPU or desktop."

473
01:27:33,440 --> 01:27:42,000
It's going to be even worse when memory prices double again, especially DRAM. Another interesting dynamic is that it's not just DRAM, it's also NAND.

474
01:27:42,000 --> 01:27:54,800
NAND is also going up in price. Both of these markets have expanded capacity very slowly over the last few years, NAND almost zero. The percentage of NAND that goes to phones and

475
01:27:54,800 --> 01:28:03,360
PCs is larger than the percentage of DRAM that goes to phones and PCs. As you destroy demand, mostly for DRAM purposes, you unlock more NAND

476
01:28:03,360 --> 01:28:13,440
that gets allocated and can go to other markets. The price increases of DRAM will be larger than those of NAND because you've released more from the consumer, and in fact,

477
01:28:13,440 --> 01:28:21,680
you've produced more memory for AI. Sorry, maybe you just explained it and I missed it. Is it because SSDs are

478
01:28:21,680 --> 01:28:32,480
being used in large quantities for data centers? They are, but not in as large quantities as DRAM. Okay, so they will also increase because they'll be using some quantity, but there's

479
01:28:32,480 --> 01:28:43,920
not as much of a need as there is for HBM. Makes sense. One thing I didn't appreciate until I was reading some of your newsletters is that the same constraints preventing logic scaling over

480
01:28:43,920 --> 01:28:55,600
the next few years are quite similar to what's preventing us from producing more memory wafers. In fact, literally the same exact machine, this EUV tool, is needed for memory.

481
01:28:55,600 --> 01:29:11,760
So I guess the question someone could ask right now is, why can't we just make more memory? The constraints, as I was mentioning earlier, are not necessarily EUV tools today or next year.

482
01:29:11,760 --> 01:29:20,000
They become that as we get to the latter part of the decade. Currently, the constraints are more that they physically just haven't built fabs.

483
01:29:20,000 --> 01:29:29,440
Over the last three to four years, these vendors have not built new fabs because memory prices were really low. Their margins were low, and in fact,

484
01:29:29,440 --> 01:29:40,560
they were losing money in 2023 on memory. So they decided they weren't building new fabs. The market slowly recovered over time but never really got amazing until last year.

485
01:29:40,560 --> 01:29:48,560
In 2024, we were banging on the drums that reasoning means long context, which means a large KV cache, which means you need a lot of memory demand.

486
01:29:48,560 --> 01:30:01,440
We've been talking about that for a year and a half, two years. People who understand AI went really long on memory then. So you’ve seen that dynamic, but now it has finally played out in pricing.

487
01:30:01,440 --> 01:30:13,440
It took so long for what was obvious: long context means the KV cache gets bigger, you need more memory. Half the cost of accelerators is memory. Of course they're going to start going crazy on it.

488
01:30:13,440 --> 01:30:20,560
It took a year for that to actually reflect in memory prices. Once memory prices reflected that, it took another three to six months for the

489
01:30:20,560 --> 01:30:34,320
memory vendors to start building fabs. Those fabs take two years to build. So we won't have really meaningful fabs to even put these tools in until late 2027 or 2028.

490
01:30:34,320 --> 01:30:47,600
Instead, you've seen some really crazy stuff to get capacity. Micron bought a fab from a company in Taiwan that makes lagging-edge chips.

491
01:30:47,600 --> 01:30:56,960
Hynix and Samsung are doing some pretty crazy things to try and expand capacity at their existing fabs, which also have large knock-on effects in the economy.

492
01:30:56,960 --> 01:31:11,840
So why can't we build more capacity? There's nowhere to put the tools. It's not just EUV; there are other tools involved in DRAM and logic. In logic, for N3, about 28%

493
01:31:11,840 --> 01:31:24,240
of the cost of the final wafer is EUV. When you look at DRAM, it's in the teens. It's going up, but it's a much smaller percentage of the cost.

494
01:31:24,240 --> 01:31:32,720
These other tools are also bottlenecks, although their supply chains are not as complex as ASML's. You see Applied Materials, Lam Research, and all these other

495
01:31:32,720 --> 01:32:40,240
companies expanding capacity a lot as well. But you don't have anywhere to put the tool, because the most complex buildings people make are fabs, and fabs take two years to build.

496
01:32:40,240 --> 01:32:53,360
I interviewed Elon recently, and his whole plan is that they're going to build this TeraFab and they're going to build the clean rooms. I won't even ask you about the dirty rooms thing,

497
01:32:53,360 --> 01:33:04,080
but let's say they build the clean rooms. I have a couple of questions. One, do you think this is the kind of thing that Elon Co. could build much

498
01:33:04,080 --> 01:33:11,440
faster than people conventionally build it? This is not about building the end tools. This is just about building the facility itself. How complicated is it to just build

499
01:33:11,440 --> 01:33:19,360
the clean room extremely fast? Is this something that Elon, with his "move fast" approach, could do much faster if that's what we're bottlenecked on this year or next year?

500
01:33:19,360 --> 01:33:29,120
Two, does that even matter if, in two years, your view is that we're not bottlenecked on clean room space, but on the tooling? As with any complex supply chain,

501
01:33:29,120 --> 01:33:40,640
it takes time, and constraints shift over time. Even if something is no longer a constraint, that doesn't mean that market no longer has margin. For example, energy will not be a big bottleneck

502
01:33:40,640 --> 01:33:47,120
a couple of years from now, but that doesn't mean energy isn't growing super fast and there's no margin there. It's just not the key bottleneck.

503
01:33:47,120 --> 01:33:57,520
In the space of fabs, clean rooms are the biggest bottleneck this year and next year. As we get to 2028, 2029, 2030, there will still be constraints there.

504
01:33:57,520 --> 01:34:08,560
The thing about Elon is he has a tremendous capability to garner physical resources and really smart people to build things. The way he recruits amazing people

505
01:34:08,560 --> 01:34:17,520
is by trying to build the craziest stuff. In the case of AI, that hasn't really worked because everyone's trying to build AGI. Everyone is very ambitious. But in the case of going to

506
01:34:17,520 --> 01:34:28,800
Mars, making rockets that land themselves, fully autonomous electric cars, or humanoid robots, these are methods of recruiting the people who think that's the most important problem in the

507
01:34:28,800 --> 01:34:35,520
world to work on that problem, because he's the only one trying really hard. In the case of semiconductors, he stated he wants to make a fab that's a million wafers per month.

508
01:34:35,520 --> 01:34:47,920
No one has a fab that big. It's possible that he's able to recruit a lot of really awesome people and get them on this crazy task of building a million wafers a month.

509
01:34:47,920 --> 01:34:58,320
Step one is to build the clean room, and that I think he probably can do. His mindset around deleting things, that it can be dirty, it's fine, is probably not right.

510
01:34:58,320 --> 01:35:11,200
Actually I think it’s 100% not right. You need the fab to be very clean. All of the air in the fab gets replaced every three seconds, it’s that fast.

511
01:35:11,200 --> 01:35:17,360
There have to be so few particles. But I think he can build the clean room. It'll take a year or two. Initially, it won't be super fast,

512
01:35:17,360 --> 01:35:26,880
but over time, he'll get faster at it. The really complex part is actually developing a process technology and building wafers. I don't think he can develop that quickly.

513
01:35:26,880 --> 01:35:39,040
That has a lot of built-up knowledge. The most complicated integration of very expensive tools and supply chains is done by TSMC, Intel, or Samsung.

514
01:35:39,040 --> 01:35:48,720
These two other companies aren't even that great at it, and they're tremendously complex. How surprised would you be if in 2030 there just happened to be some total

515
01:35:48,720 --> 01:35:56,240
disruption where we're not using EUV? What if we're using something that has much better effects, is much simpler to produce, and can be produced in much bigger quantities?

516
01:35:56,240 --> 01:36:03,760
I'm sure as an industry insider that sounds like a totally naive question, but do you see what I'm asking? What probability should we put on

517
01:36:03,760 --> 01:36:12,720
something coming totally out of left field to make all of this irrelevant? Something that's very simple and easy to scale, I assign a very, very low probability.

518
01:36:12,720 --> 01:36:21,040
There are a number of companies working on effectively particle accelerators or synchrotrons that generate light that's either 13.5 nanometer, like EUV,

519
01:36:21,040 --> 01:36:32,640
or an even narrower wavelength, like X-ray at 7 nanometers, to then use in lithography tools. But those things are massive particle accelerators generating this light.

520
01:36:32,640 --> 01:36:38,720
It's a very complicated thing to build. There are a couple of companies and I think that could be a big disruption to the industry beyond EUV.

521
01:36:38,720 --> 01:36:49,040
But I don't think we're going to magically build something new that is direct write and super simple, and can be manufactured at huge volumes, although

522
01:36:49,040 --> 01:36:59,040
there are some attempts to do things like this. I ask because if you think about Elon's companies in the past, rocketry was this thing that was thought to be—and is—incredibly complicated.

523
01:36:59,040 --> 01:37:12,000
Look, I'm just a naive yapper compared to Elon. What have I built? So maybe it's possible. In order to build more memory in the future, could we build 3D DRAM the way

524
01:37:12,000 --> 01:37:24,480
we do 3D NAND and then go back to DUV? That is the hope currently. Everyone's roadmap for 3D DRAM is that you'll still use EUV because you want to have that tighter overlay.

525
01:37:24,480 --> 01:37:33,440
When you're doing these subsequent processing steps, everything is vertically stacked and you have more layers on top of each other. You want the pitches to be tighter.

526
01:37:33,440 --> 01:37:42,240
So generally, people are still trying to do it with EUV. But what 3D would do is change the calculation of how many bits a single EUV pass can make.

527
01:37:42,240 --> 01:37:56,400
That number would go up drastically if you go to 3D DRAM. That is the hope. Right now, everyone's roadmap goes from the current 6F cell, to a 4F cell, and then finally 3D DRAM by the end

528
01:37:56,400 --> 01:38:04,400
of the decade or early next decade. There's still a lot of R&D, manufacturing, and integration to be done. I wouldn't call that out of the cards.

529
01:38:04,400 --> 01:38:14,720
I think it's very likely going to happen. It's also going to require a huge retooling of fabs. The breakdown of tools in a fab will be very different. The lithography tool is actually the

530
01:38:14,720 --> 01:38:25,120
only thing that isn't that different. But the number of them relative to different types of chemical vapor deposition, atomic layer deposition, dry etch, or different kinds of etch

531
01:38:25,120 --> 01:38:35,600
chambers with different chemistries… You have all these different tools for different process nodes. You can't just convert a logic fab to a DRAM fab, or vice versa, or a NAND fab

532
01:38:35,600 --> 01:38:46,320
to a DRAM fab, in a short amount of time. In the same way, existing DRAM fabs require a lot of retooling just to go from 1-alpha to 1-beta to 1-gamma process nodes, because they have to

533
01:38:46,320 --> 01:38:55,040
add DUV and change the chemistry stacks for when you’re using EUV in terms of deposition and etch. And the EUV tool has to be there. Furthermore, when you change to 3D DRAM,

534
01:38:55,040 --> 01:39:06,960
there's going to be an even larger shift, so a lot of retooling of these fabs needs to happen. That would be a big disruption. That would make EUV demand generally lower.

535
01:39:06,960 --> 01:39:24,560
But as we've seen across time, lithography demand as a percentage of wafer cost has trended up. Around the 2014 era, it was 17% of the wafer cost, and it's gone to 30% over the last fifteen years.

536
01:39:24,560 --> 01:39:33,840
For DRAM, it was in the low to mid-teens, and now it's trended toward the high teens. Before we get to 3D DRAM, it'll likely cross into the 20% range.

537
01:39:33,840 --> 01:39:43,760
But then, if we get to 3D DRAM, the total end wafer cost as a percentage of EUV tanks again. I guess you care less about the percent of cost and more about how much it bottlenecks production.

538
01:39:43,760 --> 01:39:56,320
Right, but the percentage of cost— It’s a proxy, yeah. If you're Jensen or Sam Altman, or whoever stands to gain a lot from scaling up AI compute,

539
01:39:56,320 --> 01:40:06,080
there are these stories that they'd go to TSMC and say, "Why can't we access Y and Z?" But I think the point you're making is that it doesn't really

540
01:40:06,080 --> 01:40:13,440
matter what TSMC does in some sense. In fact, even if you have Intel and Samsung building more foundries, in the long run, you're going to be bottlenecked

541
01:40:13,440 --> 01:40:23,920
by ASML and other tool and material makers. First, is that a correct interpretation? Second, should Silicon Valley people be going to the Netherlands right now to try

542
01:40:23,920 --> 01:40:35,520
to pitch ASML to make more tools so that in 2030 they can have more AI compute? It's a funny dynamic we saw in 2023, 2024, and 2025.

543
01:40:35,520 --> 01:40:45,120
People who saw the energy bottleneck before others asymmetrically went to Siemens, Mitsubishi, and of course GE Vernova, and bought up turbine capacity.

544
01:40:45,120 --> 01:40:52,640
Now they're able to charge excess amounts for deploying these turbines in places because of energy. In the same sense, this could be done for EUV,

545
01:40:52,640 --> 01:41:04,560
except ASML is not just going to trust any random bozo who wants to buy EUV tools. These turbines are much cheaper than EUV tools, and there's many more of them produced.

546
01:41:04,560 --> 01:41:15,360
Especially once you get to industrial gas turbines, not just combined-cycle but the cheaper, smaller, less efficient ones, people put down deposits for these. Someone could

547
01:41:15,360 --> 01:41:30,480
do this. Someone should go to the Netherlands and be like, "I'll pay you a billion dollars. You give me the right to purchase ten EUV tools two years from now, and I'm first in line."

548
01:41:30,480 --> 01:41:38,640
Then over those two years, you go around and wait for everyone to realize, "Oh crap, I don't have enough EUV tools," and you try to sell your option at some premium.

549
01:41:38,640 --> 01:41:49,680
All you're effectively doing is saying, "ASML, you're dumb. You weren't making enough margin on these. I'm going to make a margin." The question is, will ASML even agree to this? I don't think so.

550
01:41:49,680 --> 01:41:56,720
There's a world where they at least get the demand signal from that to increase production. Potentially. I agree. But it sounds like you're

551
01:41:56,720 --> 01:42:02,160
saying they couldn't even increase production if they wanted to, given the supply chain. Right. But that's exactly the market in which… If they can't increase production,

552
01:42:02,160 --> 01:42:12,640
just like TSMC cannot increase production that fast, and yet demand is mooning, then the obvious solution is to arbitrage this. You and I know demand is way higher than they're

553
01:42:12,640 --> 01:42:21,120
projecting and their capability to build. You arbitrage this by locking up the capacity, doing a forward contract, and then trying to sell it at a later date

554
01:42:21,120 --> 01:42:30,400
once other people realize everything is fucked and we don't have enough capacity. Then you'll have this insane margin that ASML and TSMC should have been charging.

555
01:42:30,400 --> 01:42:41,680
But the thing is, I don't know if ASML and TSMC will ever agree to this. Let me ask you about power now. It sounds like you think power can be arbitrarily scaled. Not arbitrarily, but yes.

556
01:42:41,680 --> 01:42:54,960
But beyond these numbers. If I'm remembering correctly, your blog post on how AI labs are increasing power implied that GE Vernova, Mitsubishi, and Siemens could

557
01:42:54,960 --> 01:43:10,000
produce 60 gigawatts a year in gas turbines. Then there are these other sources, but they're less significant than the turbines. Only a fraction of that goes to AI, I assume.

558
01:43:10,000 --> 01:43:20,880
If in 2030 we have enough logic and memory to do 200 gigawatts a year, do you just think that these things are on a path to ramp up to more than 200 gigawatts a year, or what do you see?

559
01:43:20,880 --> 01:43:29,200
Right now we're at 20 or 30. This is critical IT capacity, by the way, which is an important thing to mention. When I'm talking about these gigawatts,

560
01:43:29,200 --> 01:43:37,600
I'm talking about critical IT capacity. Server plugged in, that's how much power it pulls. But there are losses along the chain. There is loss on transmission,

561
01:43:37,600 --> 01:43:49,120
conversion, cooling, et cetera. So you should gross this factor up from 20 gigawatts for this year, or 200 gigawatts by the end of the decade, to some

562
01:43:49,120 --> 01:43:59,280
number 20-30% higher. Then you have capacity factors. Turbines don't run at 100 percent. If you look at PJM, which I think is the largest grid in America—covering the Midwest and some of

563
01:43:59,280 --> 01:44:16,720
the Northeast area—in their models they want to have roughly 20 percent excess capacity. Within that 20 percent excess capacity, they're running all the turbines at 90%

564
01:44:16,720 --> 01:44:26,960
because they are derated some for reliability, maintenance, and so on. In reality, the nameplate capacity for energy is always way higher than the actual end critical IT

565
01:44:26,960 --> 01:44:41,280
capacity because of all these factors. But it's not just turbines. If you were just making power from turbines, that's simple, boring, and easy. Humans and capitalism are far more effective.

566
01:44:41,280 --> 01:44:49,920
The whole point of that blog was that, yes, there are only three people making combined-cycle gas turbines, but there's so much more we can do. We can do aeroderivatives. We can take

567
01:44:49,920 --> 01:44:58,240
airplane engines and turn them into turbines. There are even new entrants in the market, like Boom Supersonic trying to do that and working with Crusoe.

568
01:44:58,240 --> 01:45:04,480
Also there's all the other ones like that already exist in the market. There are also medium-speed reciprocating engines: engines

569
01:45:04,480 --> 01:45:15,920
that spin in circles, like a diesel engine. There are ten people who make engines that way. I'm from Georgia, and people used to be like, "Oh man, you got a

570
01:45:15,920 --> 01:45:26,720
Cummins engine in there," regarding RAM trucks. Automobile manufacturing is going down, so these companies all have capacity and could scale and convert that for data center power.

571
01:45:26,720 --> 01:45:38,320
You stick all these reciprocating engines in. It's not as clean as combined-cycle, but maybe you can convert them from diesel to gas if you want. What about ship engines? All of these engines for

572
01:45:38,320 --> 01:45:49,440
massive cargo ships are great. Nebius is doing that for a Microsoft data center in New Jersey. They're running ship engines to generate power.

573
01:45:49,440 --> 01:45:57,840
Bloom Energy is doing fuel cells. We've been very positive on them for a year and a half now because they have such a capability to increase their production.

574
01:45:57,840 --> 01:46:06,720
Their payback period for a production increase is very fast, even if the cost is a little bit higher than combined-cycle, which is the best for cost and efficiency.

575
01:46:06,720 --> 01:46:18,960
Then there's solar plus battery, which can come online as those cost curves continue to come down. There's wind, where you might only expect 15 percent of the maximum power because things

576
01:46:18,960 --> 01:46:32,160
oscillate, but you add batteries. There are all these things. The other thing is that the grid is scaled so we don't cut off power at peak usage on the hottest day of the summer.

577
01:46:32,160 --> 01:46:41,920
But in reality, that's a load spike that is 10-20% higher than the average. If you just put enough utility-scale batteries, or peaker plants that only

578
01:46:41,920 --> 01:46:54,240
run a small portion of the year—and those could be gas, industrial gas turbines, combined-cycle, batteries, or any of the other sources I mentioned—then all of a sudden you've

579
01:46:54,240 --> 01:47:07,600
unlocked 20% of the US grid for data centers. Most of the time that capacity is sitting idle. It's really only there for that peak, which is just a few hours over a few days of the year.

580
01:47:07,600 --> 01:47:15,520
If you have enough capacity to absorb that peak load, then all of the sudden you’ve transferred it all. Today, data centers are only 3-4% of the power of

581
01:47:15,520 --> 01:47:25,840
the US grid, and by 2028 they'll be 10%. But if you can unlock 20% of the US grid like this, it's not that crazy. The US grid is terawatt-level,

582
01:47:25,840 --> 01:47:36,800
not hundreds-of-gigawatts-level. So we can add a lot more energy. I'm not saying it's easy. These things are going to be hard. There's a lot of hard engineering,

583
01:47:36,800 --> 01:47:45,920
risks people have to take, and new technologies people have to use. But Elon was the first to do this behind-the-meter gas, and since then we've seen an explosion of

584
01:47:45,920 --> 01:47:56,080
different things people are doing to get power. They're not easy, but people are gonna be able to do them. The supply chains are just way simpler than chips.

585
01:47:56,080 --> 01:48:06,000
Interesting. He made the point during the interview that for the specific blade for the specific turbine he was looking at, the lead times go out beyond 2030. Your point is that—

586
01:48:06,000 --> 01:48:17,040
That's great. There are so many other ways to make energy. Just be inefficient. It's fine. Right now, combined-cycle gas turbines have CapEx of $1,500 per kilowatt.

587
01:48:17,040 --> 01:48:24,640
Are you saying it would make sense to have either technologies that are much more expensive than that, or other things are getting cheap enough to make it competitive?

588
01:48:24,640 --> 01:48:40,320
Exactly. It can be as high as $3,500 per kilowatt. It could be twice as much as the cost of combined-cycle, and the total cost of the GPU on a TCO basis has only gone up a few cents per hour.

589
01:48:40,320 --> 01:48:54,320
Because we've been talking about Hopper pricing, $1.40, let's say the power price doubles. The Hopper that was $1.40 is now $1.50 in cost. I don't care, because the models are improving so

590
01:48:54,320 --> 01:49:06,800
fast that the marginal utility of them is worth way more than that ten-cent increase in energy. So you're saying 20 percent of the grid—the grid is about one terawatt—can just come online from

591
01:49:06,800 --> 01:49:18,480
utility-scale batteries, increasing what you'd be comfortable putting on the grid. The regulatory mechanism there is not easy, by the way. But that's 200 gigawatts, if that hypothetically happens.

592
01:49:18,480 --> 01:49:28,320
Just from the different sources of gas generation you mentioned—the different kinds of engines and turbines—combined, how many gigawatts could they unlock by the end of the decade?

593
01:49:28,320 --> 01:49:39,440
We're tracking this in our data. There are over 16 different manufacturers of power-generating things just from gas alone. Yes, there are only three turbine manufacturers

594
01:49:39,440 --> 01:49:47,920
for combined-cycle, but we're tracking 16 different vendors, and we have all of their orders. It turns out there are hundreds of

595
01:49:47,920 --> 01:49:59,760
gigawatts of orders to various data centers. As we get to the end of the decade, we think something like half of the capacity that's being added will be behind the meter.

596
01:49:59,760 --> 01:50:08,560
Behind the meter is almost always more expensive than grid-connected, but there are just a lot of problems with getting grid-connected: permits and interconnection queues and all this sort of stuff.

597
01:50:08,560 --> 01:50:17,760
So even though it's more expensive, people are doing behind the meter. What they're doing behind the meter ranges widely. It could be reciprocating engines, ship engines,

598
01:50:17,760 --> 01:50:26,880
or aeroderivatives. It could be combined-cycle, although combined-cycle is not that great for behind the meter. It could be Bloom Energy fuel cells, or solar plus battery.

599
01:50:26,880 --> 01:50:34,080
It could be any of these things. And you're saying any of these individually could do tens of gigawatts? Any of these individually will do tens of

600
01:50:34,080 --> 01:50:42,240
gigawatts, and as a whole, they will do hundreds of gigawatts. Okay. So that alone should more than— Electrician wages will probably

601
01:50:42,240 --> 01:50:51,840
double or triple again. There are going to be a lot of new people entering that field, and a ton of people who make money, but I don't see that as the main bottleneck.

602
01:50:51,840 --> 01:51:04,000
Right now in Abilene, at the 1.2-gigawatt data center that Crusoe is building for OpenAI, I think they have 5,000 people working there, or at peak they did.

603
01:51:04,000 --> 01:51:16,560
If you turn that into 100 gigawatts—and I'm sure things will get more efficient over time—that would be 400,000 people it would take to build 100 gigawatts.

604
01:51:16,560 --> 01:51:24,480
If you think about the US labor force, and how many electricians there are and how many construction workers there are… I guess there are 800,000 electricians.

605
01:51:24,480 --> 01:51:30,560
I don't know if they're all substitutable in this way. There are millions of construction workers. But if we're in a world where we're adding

606
01:51:30,560 --> 01:51:38,480
200 gigawatts a year, are we going to be crunched on labor eventually, or do you think that is actually not a real constraint? Labor is a big constraint. It's a humongous

607
01:51:38,480 --> 01:51:50,160
constraint in this. People have to be trained. Likewise, we'll probably start importing the highest-skilled labor. It makes sense that a really high-skilled

608
01:51:50,160 --> 01:52:03,760
electrician in Europe who was working on destroying power plants now comes to America and is building high-voltage electricity moving across a data center.

609
01:52:03,760 --> 01:52:13,680
Humanoid robots or robotics at least might start to help, but the main factor for reducing the number of people is going to be modularizing things and making them in factories in Asia.

610
01:52:13,680 --> 01:52:34,640
Unfortunately for America, places like Korea, Southeast Asia, and in many ways China as well are going to ship more and more built-out sections of the data center and those will be shipped in.

611
01:52:34,640 --> 01:52:43,680
Today you currently ship servers or a rack in, and then you plug that into different pieces that you're shipping from different places. But now you'll ship it to a factory

612
01:52:43,680 --> 01:52:56,880
and integrate the entire thing. Maybe this is a two-megawatt block, and this block goes from high-voltage AC power to the DC voltage that you deliver

613
01:52:56,880 --> 01:53:08,320
to the rack, or something like this. Or with cooling, you ship a fully integrated unit that has a lot of the cooling subsystems already put together,

614
01:53:08,320 --> 01:53:19,200
because plumbers are also a big constraint here. Furthermore, instead of just a single rack where you have people wiring up all these racks with electricity, you take a skid and put an entire

615
01:53:19,200 --> 01:53:32,240
row of servers on it that is shipped directly from the factories. Today, a single rack may be 120 or 140 kilowatts, but as we get to next-generation Nvidia Kyber and

616
01:53:32,240 --> 01:53:42,880
things like that, it's almost a megawatt. In addition, if you do an entire row, it'll have the rack, the networking, the cooling, and the power all integrated together.

617
01:53:42,880 --> 01:53:58,800
Now when you come in, you have much less to cable. There's less networking fiber, fewer power connections, and fewer plumbing things. This can drastically reduce the number

618
01:53:58,800 --> 01:54:08,880
of people working in data centers, so our capability to build them will be much larger. Along the way, some people will move faster to new things, and some will move slower.

619
01:54:08,880 --> 01:54:24,240
Crusoe and Google have been talking a lot about this modularization, as have companies like Meta and many others. The people who move faster to new things may

620
01:54:24,240 --> 01:54:30,880
face delays, while the people who are slower will face labor problems. There will always be dislocations in the market because this is a very complex supply chain.

621
01:54:30,880 --> 01:54:39,600
At the end of the day, it's still simple enough that we will be able to solve it through capitalism and human ingenuity on the timescales required.

622
01:54:39,600 --> 01:54:50,320
Speaking of big problems to solve, Elon Musk is very bullish on space GPUs. If you're right that power is not a constraint on Earth… I guess the other reason they would

623
01:54:50,320 --> 01:55:00,640
make sense is that even if there will be enough gas turbines or whatever on Earth, Elon's next argument is that you can't get the permitting to build hundreds of gigawatts on

624
01:55:00,640 --> 01:55:09,040
Earth. Do you buy that argument? Land-wise, America is big. Data centers don't actually take up that much space, so you can solve that.

625
01:55:09,040 --> 01:55:15,200
Permitting-wise, air pollution permits are a challenge, but the Trump administration made it much easier. You go to Texas,

626
01:55:15,200 --> 01:55:28,000
and you can skip a lot of this red tape. Elon had to deal with a lot of this complex stuff in Memphis, and then building a power plant across the border for Colossus 1 and 2.

627
01:55:28,000 --> 01:55:34,800
But at the end of the day, there's a lot more you can get away with in the middle of Texas. Given that Elon lives in Texas, why didn't he just go to Texas?

628
01:55:34,800 --> 01:55:43,760
I think it was partially that they over-indexed on grid power for a temporary period of time. That's just what they thought they needed more of. Because they had an aluminum refinery

629
01:55:43,760 --> 01:55:56,640
connected to the grid there. It was actually an idled appliance factory. But I think they may have indexed more to grid power, water access, and gas access.

630
01:55:56,640 --> 01:56:03,840
I think they bought that knowing the gas line was right there and they were going to tap it. Same with water. It was a whole host of different constraints.

631
01:56:03,840 --> 01:56:10,160
It was probably an area where electricians were easier to find. At the end of the day, I'm not exactly sure why they chose that site.

632
01:56:10,160 --> 01:56:23,840
I bet Elon would've chosen somewhere in Texas if he could've gone back because of the regulatory challenges he faced. Ultimately, permitting is a challenge,

633
01:56:23,840 --> 01:56:32,240
but America is a big place with 50 states, and things will get done. There are a lot of small jurisdictions where you can just transport in all the workers

634
01:56:32,240 --> 01:56:44,640
you need for a temporary period of three to twelve months, depending on the contractor. You can put them in temporary housing and pay out the butt, because labor is very cheap relative

635
01:56:44,640 --> 01:56:59,520
to the GPUs and the networking, and the end value of the tokens it's going to produce. So there is plenty of room to pay for all of these things.

636
01:56:59,520 --> 01:57:09,440
Also, people are also diversifying now. Australia, Malaysia, Indonesia, and India are all places where data centers are going up at a much faster pace.

637
01:57:09,440 --> 01:57:19,200
But currently, over 70% of AI data centers are still in America, and that continues to be the trend. People are figuring out how to build these things.

638
01:57:19,200 --> 01:57:30,960
Ultimately, dealing with permitting and red tape in middle-of-nowhere Texas, Wyoming, or New Mexico is probably a hell of a lot easier than sending stuff into space.

639
01:57:30,960 --> 01:57:41,920
Other than the economic argument making less sense once you consider that energy is a small fraction of the total cost of ownership of a data center, what are the other reasons you're skeptical?

640
01:57:41,920 --> 01:57:50,560
Obviously, power is basically free in space. That's the reason to do it. Yeah, that's the reason to do it. But there are all the other counterarguments.

641
01:57:50,560 --> 01:58:03,200
Even if power costs double on Earth, it's still a fraction of the total cost of the GPU. The main challenge is… We have ClusterMAX, which rates all the neoclouds.

642
01:58:03,200 --> 01:58:11,360
We test over 40 cloud companies, including the hyperscalers and neoclouds. Outside of software, what differentiates these clouds the most is their ability to deploy and

643
01:58:11,360 --> 01:58:21,240
manage failure. GPUs are horrendously unreliable. Even today, around 15% of Blackwells that get deployed have to be RMA'd. You have to take them out.

644
01:58:21,240 --> 01:58:28,240
Sometimes you just have to plug them back in, but sometimes you have to take them out and ship them back to Nvidia or their partners who do the RMAs and such.

645
01:58:28,240 --> 01:58:39,680
What do you make of Elon's argument that after an initial phase, they actually don't fail that much? Sure, but now you've done this, tested them all, deconstructed them, put them on a spaceship,

646
01:58:39,680 --> 01:58:57,680
launched them into space, and then put them online again. That takes months. If your argument is that a GPU has a useful life of five years, and this takes six additional months,

647
01:58:57,680 --> 01:59:08,960
that is 10% of your cluster's useful life. Because we're so capacity-constrained, that compute is theoretically most valuable in the first six months you have it.

648
01:59:08,960 --> 01:59:15,120
We're more constrained now than we will be in the future. That compute can contribute to a better model in the future, or generate revenue

649
01:59:15,120 --> 01:59:25,120
today that you can use to raise more money. All these things make now the most important moment, but you've potentially delayed your compute deployment by six months.

650
01:59:25,120 --> 01:59:31,280
What separates these cloud providers is… We see some clouds taking six months to deploy GPUs right here on Earth. We see clouds that take

651
01:59:31,280 --> 01:59:41,280
a lot less than six months. So the question is, where does space get in there? I don't see how you could test them all on Earth, deconstruct them, and ship them to space without

652
01:59:41,280 --> 01:59:50,000
it taking significantly longer than just leaving them in the facility where you tested them. The question I wanted to ask is about the topology of space communication.

653
01:59:50,000 --> 02:00:00,080
Right now, Starlink satellites talk to each other at 100 gigabits per second. You could imagine that being much higher with optical intersatellite

654
02:00:00,080 --> 02:00:09,200
laser links optimized for this. That actually ends up being quite close to InfiniBand bandwidth, which is 400 gigabytes a second.

655
02:00:09,200 --> 02:00:24,480
But that's per GPU, not per rack. So multiply that by 72. Also, that was Hopper. When you go to Blackwell and Rubin, that 2x's and 2x's again. But how much compute is happening per… During

656
02:00:24,480 --> 02:00:33,440
inference, are the different scale-ups still working together, or is inference just happening as a batch within a single scale-up? A lot of models fit within one scale-up domain,

657
02:00:33,440 --> 02:00:48,400
but many times you split them across multiple scale-up domains. As models become more and more sparse, which is the general trend, you want to

658
02:00:48,400 --> 02:00:59,680
ping just a couple of experts per GPU. If leading models today have hundreds, if not a thousand, of experts, then you'd want to run this across hundreds or thousands of chips,

659
02:00:59,680 --> 02:01:09,760
even as we advance into the future. So then you end up with the problem of needing to connect all these satellites together for communications as well.

660
02:01:09,760 --> 02:01:21,600
That would be tough. If there's a world where you could do inference for a batch on a single scale-up, then maybe it's more plausible. But if not, it's a different story.

661
02:01:21,600 --> 02:01:34,160
Networking these chips together is a problem, and you can't just make the satellite infinitely large. There are a lot of physics challenges to making a satellite really big. That's why you need these

662
02:01:34,160 --> 02:01:43,600
interconnects between the satellites. Those interconnects are more expensive. In a cluster, 15-20% of the cost is networking. All of a sudden, you're using space lasers

663
02:01:43,600 --> 02:01:54,160
instead of simple lasers that are manufactured in volumes of millions with pluggable transceivers. And those things are very unreliable as well, more unreliable than the GPUs by the way.

664
02:01:54,160 --> 02:01:59,680
Across the life of a cluster, you have to unplug and clean them all the time. You have to unplug and replug them just for random reasons.

665
02:01:59,680 --> 02:02:08,080
These things are just not as reliable. So you've got that problem as well. You've got a more expensive, complicated space laser to communicate instead of this

666
02:02:08,080 --> 02:02:13,840
pluggable optical transceiver that's been produced in super high volume. So all in all, what does that imply for space data centers?

667
02:02:13,840 --> 02:02:24,160
Space data centers effectively are not limited by their energy advantage. They are limited by the same contended resource. We can only make two hundred gigawatts

668
02:02:24,160 --> 02:02:36,000
of chips a year by the end of the decade. What are we going to do to get that capacity? It doesn't matter if it's on land or in space. It doesn’t really matter,

669
02:02:36,000 --> 02:02:47,360
because you can build that power. Human capabilities and capacity could get to the period where we're adding a terawatt a year globally of various types of power.

670
02:02:47,360 --> 02:02:59,040
At some point, we do cross the chasm where space data centers make sense, but it's not this decade. It is much further out, once energy constraints actually become a big bottleneck

671
02:02:59,040 --> 02:03:07,360
and land permitting becomes a much bigger bottleneck as it subsumes more of the economy. And crucially, once chips are no longer the bottleneck.

672
02:03:07,360 --> 02:03:15,920
Right now, chips are the biggest bottleneck. You want them deployed and working on AI the moment they're manufactured. There are a lot of things people are

673
02:03:15,920 --> 02:03:26,560
doing to increase that speed faster and faster. They’re modularizing data centers, or even modularizing racks where you put the chip in at the data center, but only the chip and everything

674
02:03:26,560 --> 02:03:36,400
else is already wired up and ready to go. There are things like this people are doing to decrease that time that you cannot do in space. At the end of the day, all that matters in a

675
02:03:36,400 --> 02:03:45,047
chip-constrained world is getting these chips producing tokens ASAP. Maybe by 2035, the semiconductor industry, ASML, Zeiss, and suppliers like Lam

676
02:03:45,047 --> 02:03:58,000
Research and Applied Materials and other fab manufacturers will catch up once the pendulum swings and we are able to make enough chips. Then we will be optimizing every dial and it makes

677
02:03:58,000 --> 02:04:11,680
sense to optimize the 10-15% of energy costs. As we move to ASICs potentially, and if Nvidia's margins aren't +70%, maybe that energy cost becomes 30% of the cluster.

678
02:04:11,680 --> 02:04:24,240
These are the things to optimize. But Elon doesn't win by doing 20% gains. He never wins that way. Elon wins when he swings for the fences and does 10X gains. That's what SpaceX

679
02:04:24,240 --> 02:04:37,760
is about. That's what Tesla is about. All of his success has been about that, not chasing the 20%. I think space data centers will eventually be a 10X gain as Earth's resources get more

680
02:04:37,760 --> 02:04:46,880
and more contentious, but that's not this decade. Just to drive some intuition about how much land there is on Earth… Obviously, for the chips themselves, especially if you move to a world

681
02:04:46,880 --> 02:05:01,920
where you have racks that have megawatts— That's the other thing. If manufacturing is the constraint, right now it's roughly one watt per square millimeter for AI chips.

