02:05:46,640 --> 02:05:54,320
fourth power by the Stefan-Boltzmann law. If you can run a very hot chip, it allows a lot of— No, you can't run it hotter. You can only run it denser. The problem is that getting

688
02:05:54,320 --> 02:06:05,600
the heat out of that dense area means you have to move away from standard air and liquid cooling to more exotic forms of liquid cooling, or even immersion, to get to higher power densities.

689
02:06:05,600 --> 02:06:13,760
That's more difficult in space than it is on Earth. Maybe it's worth explaining at this point what exactly a scale-up is and what it looks like for

690
02:06:13,760 --> 02:06:26,640
Nvidia versus Trainium versus TPUs. Earlier I was mentioning how communication within a chip is super fast. Communication within chips that are in the

691
02:06:26,640 --> 02:06:36,560
same rack is fast, but not as fast. It's on the order of terabytes. Communication very far away is on the order of hundreds of gigabytes.

692
02:06:36,560 --> 02:06:44,320
As you get further distance, maybe across the country, the order of magnitude is on the order of gigabytes. A scale-up domain is this tight domain

693
02:06:44,320 --> 02:06:55,040
where the chips are communicating on the order of terabytes a second. For Nvidia, previously this meant an H100 server had eight GPUs,

694
02:06:55,040 --> 02:07:03,360
and those eight GPUs could talk to each other at terabytes a second. With Blackwell NVL72, they implemented rack-scale scale-up.

695
02:07:03,360 --> 02:07:13,200
That meant all seventy-two GPUs in the rack could connect to each other at terabytes a second. The speed doubled generation on generation, but the most important innovation was going from eight

696
02:07:13,200 --> 02:07:20,960
to seventy-two in the domain. When we look at Google, their scale-up domain is completely different. It has always been on the order of thousands.

697
02:07:20,960 --> 02:07:31,040
With TPU v4, they had pods the size of four thousand chips. With v8 or v7, they have pods in the eight or nine thousand range.

698
02:07:31,040 --> 02:07:40,080
What's relevant here is that it's not the same as Nvidia. It's not like for like. Google has a topology that's a torus. Every chip connects to six neighbors.

699
02:07:40,080 --> 02:07:52,080
Nvidia's 72 GPUs connect all-to-all. They can send terabytes a second to any arbitrary other chip in that pod of scale-up. Whereas Google, you have to bounce through chips.

700
02:07:52,080 --> 02:08:04,800
If TPU 1 needs to talk to TPU 76, it has to bounce through various chips, and there is always some blocking of resources when you do that because that one TPU is only connected to six other TPUs.

701
02:08:04,800 --> 02:08:11,200
So there is a difference in topology and bandwidth, and there are trade-offs and advantages to both. Google gets to have a massive scale-up domain,

702
02:08:11,200 --> 02:08:23,200
but they have the trade-off of bouncing across chips to get from one to another. You can only talk to six direct neighbors. Amazon has mutated their scale-up domain.

703
02:08:23,200 --> 02:08:33,007
They're somewhere in between Nvidia and Google. They're trying to make larger scale-up domains. They try to do all-to-all to some extent with switches, which is what Nvidia does, but they also

704
02:08:33,007 --> 02:08:44,720
use torus topologies like Google to some extent. As we advance forward to next generations, all three of them are moving more towards a dragonfly topology.

705
02:08:44,720 --> 02:08:54,720
That means there are some fully connected elements and some elements that are not fully connected. You can get the scale-up to be hundreds or thousands of chips, but also have it not contend

706
02:08:54,720 --> 02:09:08,160
for resources when bouncing through chips. Related question: I heard somebody make the claim that the reason parameter scaling has been slow—and only now are we getting bigger models

